#lang saffron
;; any-widen-frame-box-past-dyn-call -- a DYNAMIC call somewhere in a body must
;; not disqualify the parameters that dynamic call never sees.
;;
;; The frame-box rule (any-widen-frame-box) asks one question of the callee: does
;; this body retain a pointer its `any` parameter carries?  The walk answering it
;; refused outright on `EX_DYN_CALL` -- the callee is a value, so there is no body
;; to inspect and no mask to consult.
;;
;; That is a sound answer about a value FLOWING INTO a dynamic call, and the
;; wrong answer about the mere presence of one.  A dynamic call that never
;; mentions the parameter is handed nothing of it to keep.  Refusing on presence
;; disqualified every parameter of every body containing a dynamic call, which in
;; Saffron is every higher-order function: a walker's `xs` read as retained
;; because a sibling subexpression called `(g h)`, where `g` and `h` are other
;; bindings entirely.  Each call site then heap-copied the list header into a box
;; nothing owned -- one malloc per call, in the language where the widen is the
;; common case rather than the corner.
;;
;; The walk checks the dynamic call's OPERANDS instead, unconfined.  `xs` under
;; one is still refused (see `keeps`, below); `xs` merely co-located with one is
;; not.
;;
;; The match binders these walkers forward do not make the parameter escape: the
;; arm lowering deref-COPIES the scrutinee and binds each field by value, so a
;; binder carries a pointer to a SIBLING allocation, never into the parameter's
;; own box -- which is the box the frame-box rule relocates.
;;
;; requires.leak-check is the whole point: the loop count is what makes one
;; missing free visible, and a frame box that was wrongly admitted would show up
;; there as a use-after-free rather than a leak.
;;
;; Every walker here returns a SCALAR on purpose.  A walker that returns a new
;; LIST widens in RETURN position, and that box is the residue
;; saffron-static-ownership-residue tracks -- including it would make the gate
;; permanently red and say nothing about the argument-position rule this pins.
;;
;; Measured 2026-10-21, before the walk changed: 2500 allocations leaked over
;; the 500 turns below.  After: none.

(defdata Lst [] (Cons [hd : any tl : any]) (Nil))

;; The `lfold` shape: an accumulator threaded through a dynamic call, beside a
;; recursive call forwarding the tail binder.  `xs` itself reaches neither.
(defn lfold2 [g acc xs]
  (match xs
    (Cons h t) (lfold2 g (g acc h) t)
    (Nil)      acc))

;; The `lmap` shape -- a dynamic call on the HEAD binder rather than on the
;; accumulator -- reduced to a scalar so the walk is pinned without the
;; return-position widen.
(defn count-if [p xs]
  (match xs
    (Cons h t) (+ (if (p h) 1 0) (count-if p t))
    (Nil)      0))

;; The counter-case -- `xs` itself an operand of the dynamic call, which must
;; STILL be refused -- is pinned next door in
;; any-widen-frame-box-dyn-call-operand.  It leaks by construction (a refused
;; widen has no owner: saffron-static-ownership-residue), so it cannot share a
;; leak-clean gate with the shapes here.
(defn add [a b] (+ a b))
(defn big? [x] (> x 1))

;; The recursive call is deliberately NOT in tail position, so this fixture's
;; gate is about the frame-box rule and nothing else; the tail-position spine
;; drop is byval-spine-drop-past-early-exit's.
(defn drive [i : int n : int] : int
  (if (= i n)
    0
    (let [xs (Cons 1 (Cons 2 (Cons 3 (Nil))))]
      (+ (+ (cast (lfold2 add 0 xs) int)
            (cast (count-if big? xs) int))
         (drive (+ i 1) n)))))

(defn main [] : int
  ;; 1+2+3 = 6, and two of the three are > 1, so 8 a turn.
  (println (drive 0 500))
  0)
